# Reading a loom in a page

An editor that runs in a browser draws a loom while its author types: a heading set apart, an anchor as a link, a fault underlined. It has to learn where those things stand. It can learn it from a grammar of its own, or from Loom. A second grammar drifts from the first, as every copy does. So a page reads a loom with the passes of this part, the same passes the command line and the language server run, and it gets the document they get.

A page can run them because reading a loom needs nothing a browser lacks. The passes take a text and give a tree. They open no file, start no process, and ask nothing of the operating system. The builder of the chapter "Building one module" reads through a `Source` its caller hands it, so a page hands it the text its author has typed.

## What a page may import

The entry `browser` names what a page may import from this package. It gives the builder of one module, `LoomCorpusAstBuilder`, with the `Source` it reads through and the `ReadError` and `ParsedLoom` it speaks of. It also gives the passes that builder runs, one by one, for a page that wants a single step: the line ranges, the classifier, the tokeniser, the builder of the tree, and the row of small passes with `nothingDeclared`, what a workspace with no trope declares.

The rest of the package stays out of the entry. The compiler, the tangler and the language server reach for the file system, for child processes and for the hash functions of Node, and a page that imported one of them would fail when it called into it.

=>

export { LoomSourceRanges } from '#ast/LineRanges'
export { WeftClassifier } from '#ast/WeftClassifier'
export { WeftTokeniser } from '#ast/WeftTokeniser'
export { LoomAstBuilder } from '#ast/LoomAstBuilder'
export { type Declared, LoomDocumentBuilder, nothingDeclared } from '#ast/LoomDocumentBuilder'
export {
  LoomCorpusAstBuilder,
  type ParsedLoom,
  ReadError,
  type Source,
} from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'

## The smallest page

The tests need a page to bundle. This one is the smallest that reads a loom. It imports the entry, builds one module from a text held in memory, and gives the module back as a promise. A page is where a program in a browser starts, so the page is what runs the Effect.

The function `moduleOfText` takes the path a loom goes by and the loom's text. Its source answers every read with that text and lists no other loom, so the builder sees a corpus of one.

=>

import { Effect, Option } from 'effect'
import type { LoomModule } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { LoomCorpusAstBuilder } from '../../src/browser'

export const moduleOfText = (path: string, text: string): Promise<LoomModule> =>
  Effect.runPromise(
    LoomCorpusAstBuilder.pipe(
      Effect.flatMap((builder) =>
        builder.build({ read: () => Effect.succeed(text), list: Option.none() }, path),
      ),
      Effect.provide(LoomCorpusAstBuilder.layer),
    ),
  )

## Bundling it as a browser loads it

A browser has none of the modules built into Node. Bun's bundler does not refuse an import of one when it bundles for a browser. It puts an empty stand-in where the module would be, and the page fails later, at the first call into it. So a bundle that builds proves nothing, and the tests watch the imports themselves.

The function `reachedBy` bundles a list of entries for a browser, and watches every import the bundler resolves on the way. An import whose name `builtIn` knows is one a browser cannot serve: a module built into Node or into Bun, by its bare name or behind `node:` or `bun:`. The bundler reports each import through a callback, so the watch gathers what it hears in a list of its own. Each one it gathers is a `Reached`: the built-in module, and the file that asked for it, written from the repository's root. The stand-in Bun puts in a built-in module's place imports other built-in modules of its own. The watch leaves those out, so the list names files of this repository alone.

The function `bundleOf` bundles one entry with no watch, and gives the code a page loads. The two are apart because a watch changes the bundle. While a plugin stands in the way of its resolving, Bun's bundler drops less of the code nothing uses, and the bundle comes out nearly a third heavier than the one a server sends.

The entries the tests bundle are two. One is the smallest page of the section before. The other is every module `@athrio/loom-ast` exports, read from that package's manifest, since a page draws its colours and its links from the model too, and a module added to the model is then watched from the day it is exported.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { builtinModules } from 'node:module'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { join, relative, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { moduleOfText } from './browser/page'

const root = resolve(import.meta.dir, '../../..')

const page = join(import.meta.dir, 'browser', 'page.ts')

const modelPackage = join(root, 'packages', 'loom-ast')

const modelEntries: ReadonlyArray<string> = Object.values(
  (
    JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(modelPackage, 'package.json'), 'utf8')) as {
      readonly exports: Readonly<Record<string, string>>
    }
  ).exports,
).map((file) => join(modelPackage, file))

type Reached = { readonly module: string; readonly from: string }

const builtIns = new Set(builtinModules)

const builtIn = (name: string): boolean =>
  name === 'bun' || name.startsWith('bun:') || name.startsWith('node:') || builtIns.has(name)

const reachedBy = async (entries: ReadonlyArray<string>): Promise<ReadonlyArray<Reached>> => {
  const reached: Array<Reached> = []
  await Bun.build({
    entrypoints: [...entries],
    target: 'browser',
    format: 'esm',
    plugins: [
      {
        name: 'the built-in modules a page reaches',
        setup: (build) => {
          build.onResolve({ filter: /.*/ }, ({ path, importer }) => {
            if (builtIn(path) && importer.startsWith(root))
              reached.push({ module: path, from: relative(root, importer) })
            return undefined
          })
        },
      },
    ],
  })
  return reached
}

const bundleOf = async (entry: string, minify: boolean): Promise<string> => {
  const built = await Bun.build({ entrypoints: [entry], target: 'browser', format: 'esm', minify })
  const code = await Promise.all(built.outputs.map((output) => output.text()))
  return code.join('\n')
}

## A page reads what the command line reads

The first case bundles the page and the whole model, and expects the watch to have gathered nothing. When a pass the entry gives grows an import of the file system, this case fails and names the file that asked for it.

The second case loads the bundle and reads a loom with it: the chapter of this part on the classifier, which holds prose, code, anchors and a sink. It reads the same text again with the builder outside any bundle, as the command line runs it, and expects the two modules to be the same, written out whole.

A page loads the bundle before it can draw its first mark, so the last case weighs it: minified and compressed, as a server sends it. The bundle weighs about 65 kilobytes. Loom's own passes are a little over half of that, and Effect is the rest. The budget is 100, so the case fails when a new import brings a library with it, and it prints the weight on every run.

=>

describe('a page that reads a loom', () => {
  const chapter = join(root, 'corpus', '03-reading-the-text', '02-classifying-lines.loom')

  it('reaches no module built into Node or into Bun', async () => {
    expect(await reachedBy([page, ...modelEntries])).toEqual([])
  })

  it('builds the module the command line builds', async () => {
    const file = join(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-page-')), 'page.js')
    writeFileSync(file, await bundleOf(page, false))
    const loaded = (await import(file)) as { readonly moduleOfText: typeof moduleOfText }
    const text = readFileSync(chapter, 'utf8')
    const inPage = await loaded.moduleOfText(chapter, text)
    const outside = await moduleOfText(chapter, text)
    expect(inPage.doc.sections.length).toBeGreaterThan(5)
    expect(JSON.stringify(inPage)).toBe(JSON.stringify(outside))
  })

  it('weighs less than a page should wait for', async () => {
    const weight = Bun.gzipSync(Buffer.from(await bundleOf(page, true))).length
    console.log(`size: a page that reads a loom loads ${Math.round(weight / 1024)} KB, compressed`)
    expect(weight).toBeLessThan(100 * 1024)
  })
})

